<%# One category and the rules that claim for it. Locals: `group` (BudgetPagePresenter::Group)
    and `groups` (the whole give-way order — the card is a row of that drag list).

    THE LIST IS THE PAGE, WHERE IT USED TO BE ONE ACCOUNT'S BAND (two-ledger spec §2). Priority was
    compared only inside an account while a fill walked `account.child_pools.by_priority`;
    `Category.in_fill_order` ranks every holder against every other over one list now, so `groups`
    is the whole order and the scope of one reorder.

    The header says what the category CLAIMS (computed-claims §3) in the same words Home's hero uses
    for the total of them — the same red for the same two facts. A second vocabulary here would have
    two screens describe one category differently on the same afternoon.

    `priority` is stated because this page is where it is set; the number is what decides who
    GIVES WAY first when the money runs out (computed-claims §4 — Home's shortfall walks this order
    backwards), and dragging this card — or its ▲▼ — is what writes it. Since rules carry a TYPE
    (rules-own-the-budget §3) it decides that WITHIN a type: the label on each row below says which,
    and the overview at the top of the page states what each kind asks of a period. %>
<section class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded"
         data-category-group="<%= group.category.name %>"
         data-category-id="<%= group.category.id %>"
         data-app--budget-page--reorder-target="row"
         draggable="true"
         data-action="dragstart->app--budget-page--reorder#start
                      dragend->app--budget-page--reorder#finish">
  <%# STACKS BELOW `sm` (design review H5). Four things wanted this row — two 44px arrows, the
      category's name, its priority and a status clause that runs to "$400.00 left · last period" —
      and at 375px the status wrapped under the arrows and the name truncated to a syllable. The
      handle and the name keep the first line; the status takes the second, indented past the
      arrows so it still reads as belonging to this category.

      `items-center` and not `items-baseline`, because the arrows are now 44px tall (M4) and a
      baseline-aligned 44px control drags the whole row's text to its bottom edge. %>
  <div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-200 flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:justify-between sm:items-center gap-1 sm:gap-4">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 min-w-0">
      <%= render "reorder_controls", group: group, groups: groups %>
      <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 truncate"><%= group.category.name %></h3>
      <span class="text-xs text-gray-500 shrink-0">priority <%= group.priority %></span>
    </div>
    <%# ** WHAT THE CATEGORY CLAIMS (computed-claims §3), WHERE THE STATUS USED TO BE. ** The header
        rendered `shared/_holding_status` — `$400.00 left · last period — you changed a rule here
        after distributing` — and every clause of that is about money MOVED into the category and
        about the distribution that moved it. Nothing moves (§5): there is no balance, no swept
        period and no distribution. `Σ its rules' claims` is what the category has, it is what `free`
        subtracted on Home, and the rows beneath spell out where it came from.

        RED WHEN A RULE UNDER IT NEEDS A HUMAN — the same two facts Home's trouble strip fires on
        (over, overdue), asked of the same rows, so the two screens cannot disagree about one
        category on one afternoon. %>
    <span class="text-sm <%= group.needs_attention? ? "text-status-danger font-medium" : "text-gray-700" %>"
          data-category-claim>
      <%= number_to_currency(group.claim) %> claimed
    </span>
  </div>

  <% group.rules.each do |rule| %>
    <%= render "rule_row", rule: rule %>
  <% end %>
</section>
